A JavaScript Promise represents a value that will be available now, later, or never. It has three states: pending, fulfilled (resolved with a value), or rejected (with an error).

```js
const p = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
  setTimeout(() => resolve("Success!"), 1000);
});

p.then(result => console.log(result))
 .catch(err => console.error(err));
```

Use `.then()` to handle success, `.catch()` for errors, and `.finally()` for cleanup after completion.
